\pard\sa240\qc\b\f0\fs32 SolarEdge Dashboard Architecture Specification\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa120\qc\f0 Version 1.0\par
\pard\sa240\qc\f0 Date: 2026-05-19\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 1. Purpose\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\f0 This document defines a target software architecture for the SolarEdge dashboard so historical energy data can be stored in a free online database, reused by the web application, and queried efficiently without depending on a live SolarEdge API call for every page load.\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 2. Objectives\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa120\f0 1. Persist energy history online at low or zero cost.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 2. Keep the SolarEdge API key server-side only.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 3. Support intraday, daily, weekly, monthly, and yearly charts.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 4. Reduce dependency on SolarEdge availability during dashboard browsing.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 5. Support future analytics such as self-consumption, import/export, and rollups.\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 3. Current Application Context\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa120\f0 The current application is a Node.js and Express web application that retrieves SolarEdge data, aggregates it on the server, and renders charts in the browser. It currently includes a frontend dashboard, an Express API layer, SolarEdge client code, and chart rendering logic.\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 4. Architectural Principle\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\f0 The system should move from a request-time integration model to a persisted data model:\par

\pard\sa120\qc\b\f0 SolarEdge API -> Ingestion Job -> Online Database -> API Layer -> Dashboard UI\b0\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 5. Candidate Storage Proposals\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa120\b\f0 Proposal A: Supabase (Recommended)\b0\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Free PostgreSQL-based platform. Best fit for time-series storage, SQL aggregations, rollups, and long-term growth. Good balance between cost, flexibility, and maintainability.\par

\pard\sa120\b\f0 Proposal B: Turso / libSQL\b0\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Lightweight cloud SQLite approach. Simpler than PostgreSQL and suitable for a personal dashboard. Good option if minimal operational overhead is preferred.\par

\pard\sa120\b\f0 Proposal C: Firebase Firestore\b0\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Managed and free-tier friendly, but less natural for time-series analytics and aggregated reporting. Better for app-platform use cases than analytical dashboards.\par

\pard\sa120\b\f0 Proposal D: GitHub Actions + JSON Snapshots\b0\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Lowest-cost alternative. Scheduled jobs generate data snapshots stored in files. Acceptable for static reporting, but weaker for querying arbitrary intervals and long-term evolution.\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 6. Recommended Target Architecture\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Recommended platform: Supabase.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 Recommended system shape:\par
\pard\sa120\f0 1. Frontend dashboard remains static and browser-based.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 2. Node.js API remains the only backend entry point for the UI.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 3. A scheduled ingestion service fetches SolarEdge energy data at regular intervals.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 4. Raw samples are stored in a database table.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 5. Daily and monthly rollups are computed from raw samples.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 6. The dashboard reads primarily from the database, not directly from SolarEdge.\par
\pard\sa120\f0 7. Live power flow may still be requested from SolarEdge at runtime for near-real-time values.\par

\pard\sa220\b\f0\fs24 7. Application and Data Flow Diagram\b0\fs22\par
